nodecode-gatekeeper is a rewrite of percyhendrux123-prog's percyhendrux123-prog/hermes-gatekeeper:

  https://github.com/percyhendrux123-prog/hermes-gatekeeper
  commit e6fb3d7547e5398df9ef72418ee4441386386c3a
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Percy Keith Stewart (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the policy itself, in Common Lisp, and nothing of the
original's Python. The rules are the same and decide in the same order -- an
absolute STOP suppression, a per-channel consent record, a recipient-local
permitted window, an unanswered thread and a minimum gap between sends, and
exact wording templates filled from stored variables -- with the same three
outcomes, and the ledger keeps hashes of recipients and message bodies rather
than the text. That ledger was SQLite with owner-only permissions; here it is
one owner-only s-expression file under the Nodecode home. What was left out:
the Hermes host contract that made the original a gate rather than an adviser
-- its pre_tool_call hook, which parsed literal `hermes send` and `imsg send`
terminal commands and its own send_message shape, its post_tool_call
observation that closed a thread by itself, and its post_gateway_admission hook
that watched inbound traffic for STOP words and direct-message replies. A
Nodecode cell reaches no Hermes send command, so the same policy is offered on
two tools the model calls around a message (gatekeeper_check and
gatekeeper_record) and on /gatekeeper for the owner, and nothing enforces a
message that never asks. The IANA timezone name of a consent record became a
UTC offset, because a rewrite without a zone database does not follow a
daylight-saving change. The original's cronjob and send-looking-tool escalation
vocabulary has no counterpart here and was not carried over.
